              "inline_data": null,
              "text": "Whether **ambrosia** is a food or a drink depends on the context, though it is most commonly categorized as a **food**. \n\n### 1. In Greek Mythology\nIn ancient Greek myths, ambrosia is the divine substance of the gods on Mount Olympus that grants them youth and immortality. \n* **Usually Food:** Traditionally, **ambrosia** is described as the **food** of the gods, while **nectar** is their **drink**. \n* **The Exception:** The distinction was not always strict. Some ancient writers used the terms interchangeably. For example, the famous Greek poet Sappho described ambrosia as a drink (comparing it to a divine wine) and nectar as the food. \n\n### 2. In Modern Culinary Terms\nIn modern times, **ambrosia** (often called *ambrosia salad*) is definitely a **food**. It is a traditional American sweet salad (popular in the Southern United States) typically made with mandarin oranges, pineapple chunks, shredded coconut, and miniature marshmallows, all folded into a base of whipped cream, cool whip, or sour cream.\n\n### Summary\n* **In ancient mythology:** It is traditionally the **food** of the gods (paired with nectar, the drink), though the Greeks occasionally swapped the two.\n* **In a modern kitchen:** It is a sweet, creamy fruit **salad (food)**.",
